{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "440",
    "case_summary": "A 40-year-old woman presented with abdominal pain and constipation. She had been evaluated 3 months earlier for 3-4 months of amenorrhea. She had a history of thyroid condition. During the previous evaluation, thyroid-stimulating hormone was undetectable, and she was prescribed methimazole. She continued to have amenorrhea and reported abdominal fullness and constipation.\n\nVital signs were unremarkable. Physical examination revealed a tender midline abdominal mass.\n\n- Initial Tests:    * Point-of-care (POC) urine human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) test: negative    * Thyroid-stimulating hormone: undetectable    * Radioiodine thyroid uptake study was performed      - Follow-up Tests:    * Serum hCG: 74,503 mIU/mL    * Ultrasonography: Confirmed live intrauterine pregnancy at 24 weeks",
